CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure contains four panels, (a) through (d), that illustrate the trigonal bipyramidal electron-pair geometry and identify the energetically preferred arrangement of lone pairs in ClF3. Panel (a) shows an unlabeled trigonal bipyramidal frame with arrows identifying the two types of positions: the "Axial" positions at the top and bottom of a vertical axis, and the "Equatorial" positions arranged in a triangular plane around the middle. Panels (b), (c), and (d) each show a Cl atom at the center of the trigonal bipyramidal geometry with three F atoms (labeled F) and two lone pairs (shown as short red bond lines) distributed among the five positions in three distinct arrangements: panel (b) places both lone pairs in equatorial positions, producing the T-shaped molecular structure that is actually observed; panel (c) places one lone pair axial and one equatorial; and panel (d) places both lone pairs in axial positions, the arrangement with the greatest lone-pair–bond-pair repulsion. This figure establishes that lone pairs preferentially occupy the more spacious equatorial positions — where 120° bond angles reduce repulsion — and that this preference uniquely determines the experimentally observed T-shaped geometry of ClF3 within VSEPR theory.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Trigonal bipyramidal wireframe: two apical vertices on a vertical axis plus three equatorial vertices on a horizontal triangle, all connected by olive-green edges
  - Translucent light-green shaded equatorial triangle (plane through the three equatorial vertices), with its rear edge drawn as a dashed green line
  - Gray interior bond lines from the central position to the vertices (used where a position holds a normal bond or is unoccupied)
  - Red short bond lines from the center to the vertices representing lone pairs
  - Central atom label 'Cl' at the origin of each panel-(b/c/d) structure
  - Peripheral atom labels 'F' placed just outside the occupied vertices
  - Panel (a) black leader lines with elbow: vertical line labelled 'Equatorial' pointing to the left equatorial vertex, horizontal line labelled 'Axial' pointing to the top axial vertex
  - Vertical dashed black separator lines between panels
  - Panel captions '(a)', '(b)', '(c)', '(d)' centered below each panel
  - In panels (b)-(d): a left structure (electron-pair geometry with red lone pairs) and a right structure (same frame relabelled with F atoms showing the resulting molecular shape)

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
One trigonal bipyramid: central atom at origin; axial vertices at (0, +1.7, 0) and (0, -1.7, 0); three equatorial vertices at radius 1.35 in the y=0 plane, oriented as in the source so that one points toward the viewer-and-down on screen (azimuth -90°, i.e. +Z toward viewer) and the other two recede left-back and right-back (azimuth 150° and 30°). The rear edge joining the two back equatorial vertices is DASHED; the two front edges are solid. Equatorial triangle filled translucent light green (#DCE9B8, ~45% opacity) with olive-green outline #8CC63F / #7FAF37 (line width ~2.5). Interior bonds from center to vertices are gray #9A9A9A; lone-pair bonds are dark red #A0342A and drawn slightly SHORTER (~70% of the full radius) than the gray bonds. Labels black, sans-serif, ~0.35 units tall. Panels (b)-(d) each contain TWO bipyramids side by side, centers 4.2 units apart in X; the left one carries the 'Cl' label plus red lone-pair lines, the right one carries 'Cl' at center and 'F' labels outside its three occupied vertices. Panel (a) is a single unlabelled frame with black leader lines: a vertical black line from above-left bending horizontally into the LEFT-BACK equatorial vertex, labelled 'Equatorial' at its top; a short horizontal black line entering the TOP AXIAL vertex from the right, labelled 'Axial'. Vertical black dashed separators between panels; caption '(a)'…'(d)' about 2.6 units below each panel center. Arrangement per source image: (b) lone pairs on the right-back and front equatorial vertices → F at top axial, bottom axial, left-back equatorial (T-shaped); (c) lone pairs on BOTH axial vertices (red line running top vertex through center to bottom vertex) → F on all three equatorial vertices; (d) lone pairs on the top axial vertex and the right-back equatorial vertex → F at left-back equatorial, front equatorial, bottom axial. Angles for the repulsion overlay: axial–equatorial = 90°, equatorial–equatorial = 120°, axial–axial = 180°; 90° LP–BP counts are 4 (b), 6 (c), 5 (d).

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitFrame",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The trigonal bipyramid is two distinct position sets: three equatorial vertices spread 120° apart in one plane, two axial vertices 90° above and below that plane — a difference invisible in a single flat drawing."
  },
  {
    "id": "positionType",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "Clicking the 'Axial' or 'Equatorial' label in panel (a) highlights that position set in every panel at once, so the learner can read any arrangement as 'which type of site does each lone pair occupy'."
  },
  {
    "id": "panel",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Panel",
    "options": [
      "(a) axial vs equatorial positions",
      "(b) both lone pairs equatorial — observed T-shape",
      "(c) both lone pairs axial",
      "(d) one axial, one equatorial"
    ],
    "default": "(a) axial vs equatorial positions",
    "teaches": "Cycling the three candidate ClF3 arrangements shows that only the placement of the two lone pairs changes, and that each placement yields a different molecular shape (T-shaped, trigonal-planar-like, or bent-axial)."
  },
  {
    "id": "showRepulsions",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Show 90° lone-pair–bond-pair repulsions",
    "default": false,
    "teaches": "Counting the 90° lone-pair–bond-pair contacts (4 for panel b, 6 for panel c, 5 for panel d) explains quantitatively why equatorial lone pairs are preferred and why panel (b) is the real geometry of ClF3."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Two kinds of positions",
    "narration": "The trigonal bipyramid offers two axial sites on the vertical axis and three equatorial sites in the shaded horizontal plane. Orbit the frame: equatorial neighbours sit 120° apart, axial-to-equatorial angles are only 90°.",
    "state": {
      "panel": "(a) axial vs equatorial positions",
      "showRepulsions": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "(b) Both lone pairs equatorial",
    "narration": "Placing both lone pairs (red) in the roomy equatorial plane leaves F atoms at the two axial sites and one equatorial site — the T-shaped structure actually observed for ClF3.",
    "state": {
      "panel": "(b) both lone pairs equatorial — observed T-shape",
      "showRepulsions": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "(c) Both lone pairs axial",
    "narration": "Forcing both lone pairs onto the axis puts each of them 90° from all three bonding pairs — six close lone-pair–bond-pair contacts, the most repulsive option.",
    "state": {
      "panel": "(c) both lone pairs axial",
      "showRepulsions": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "(d) One axial, one equatorial",
    "narration": "A mixed arrangement gives five 90° lone-pair–bond-pair contacts — better than (c) but still worse than (b).",
    "state": {
      "panel": "(d) one axial, one equatorial",
      "showRepulsions": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Why T-shaped wins",
    "narration": "Return to (b) and click the 'Equatorial' label: lone pairs always seek the equatorial sites where 120° separation minimises repulsion, which uniquely fixes the T-shaped geometry of ClF3.",
    "state": {
      "panel": "(b) both lone pairs equatorial — observed T-shape",
      "showRepulsions": false
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.6
}

NOTES: Build each bipyramid as a reusable Group: LineSegments (or thick Line2) for the 10 outer edges with the single rear equatorial edge as a LineDashedMaterial (call computeLineDistances), a ShapeGeometry/MeshBasicMaterial triangle with transparent:true, depthWrite:false, side:DoubleSide for the shaded equatorial plane, and per-vertex bond cylinders/lines whose material swaps between gray and red based on the current panel state. Labels (Cl, F, Equatorial, Axial, panel captions) as CSS2DObject or sprite text so they stay screen-facing and legible while orbiting. Panel switching should crossfade or just rebuild bond colors on the same shared frame so the learner sees only the lone pairs move. Highlight mode from the click interaction: emissive/thicker outline plus a soft colored halo on the two axial or three equatorial vertices in every visible structure simultaneously. Repulsion overlay: thin translucent arcs (TorusGeometry segments or line arcs) between each lone pair and each 90°-adjacent bond, with a count badge. Default camera is a standard isometric pose, slight tilt above the equatorial plane so the plane reads as an ellipse; orbit limited in polar angle to keep the vertical axis upright. Flat white background, no shadows, no specular — match the line-art look of the textbook.

Follow the interaction plan above.

